  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1)\n\nA mid level closed high remains in place across the southeastern\nstates. For Wednesday and Thursday, the warm sector associated with\nthis high will be characterized by dewpoints reaching the lower 70s\nand high temperatures in the mid 80s to lower 90s. This could support\nheat indices reaching advisory criteria (heat index of 100 or \ngreater) for the southern sections of the ILN CWA. However, \nconfidence remains low because of the potential for increasing clouds\n(and maybe even convection) which could keep temperatures a little \nbelow the current forecast.\n\nThe convective forecast through the next few days remains similar to\nprevious forecasts. A generally east-to-west frontal zone will be \nfocused just north of the ILN CWA through the next couple days, \nthough it may oscillate somewhat northward or southward from time to \ntime. Given the placement of this front, convective chances Wednesday\nand Thursday appear like they will be focused in the northern \nsections of the ILN CWA, at least initially. Storms will have the \npotential to propagate southward into the warmer air, but if they do,\nthey will become more displaced from the forcing and the greater \nmid-level wind flow. Overall, with marginal shear and narrow CAPE \nprofiles, severe weather appears unlikely. A few strong to possibly \nsevere storms could occur, but an organized threat appears unlikely. \nThe greater threat will likely be for heavy rain, as precipitable \nwater values will be around 2 inches or even slightly greater. The \neast-west boundary will also support the potential for several rounds\nof storms over repeated locations. WPC has included much of the \nforecast area in a MRGL risk of excessive rainfall for both Wednesday\nand Thursday.\n\nAs the ridge breaks down on Friday, the frontal zone will shift\nfarther south into the Ohio Valley -- lowering max temps, but\nincreasing precipitation chances. Forecast confidence becomes lower\nin any specifics from Friday onward, but with additional shortwave\nenergy developing upstream, some chances for showers and storms will\nlikely continue into the weekend.",
